  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ y=x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+1 & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =x \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,0).$\n\n- Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =(1)+1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,0)$ and $(1,2).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-1,q=0,r=1$ and $s=2,$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-1+0+1+2=2.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14267214/assets/3a2110013e2d2c481a0b220d3cbe7ebbf2adbd249039ed185233e37fcd3b220d.png)",
